<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Fulgencio Batista</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1901">1901</a>–<a href="/years/1973">1973</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Fulgencio Batista was a Cuban army sergeant who rose to power after the revolution of 1933 and dominated Cuban politics for most of the next quarter-century. He was elected president in 1940 and seized power again in a coup in 1952. His corrupt <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dictatorship">dictatorship</a> was overthrown by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fidel-castro">Fidel Castro</a>'s revolutionaries, and he fled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuba">Cuba</a> on 1 January 1959.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/fulgencio-batista.jpg" alt="Fulgencio Batista" width="320" height="348" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Fulg%C3%AAncio_Batista_e_esposa,_1955_(cropped).tif" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Fulgencio Batista y Zaldívar was born on 16 January 1901 in Veguita, in the municipality of Banes, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuba">Cuba</a>, to parents who had fought in the Cuban War of Independence. He was of Spanish, African and Chinese descent, and possibly of some <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taino">Taíno</a> descent. His mother named him Rubén and gave him her surname, and he was legally Rubén Zaldívar until 1939. When he then tried to register as a presidential candidate, no birth certificate existed for Fulgencio Batista, and he had to pay the local judge 15,000 pesos.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Batista joined the Sergeants' Revolt of 1933, which overthrew <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gerardo-machado">Gerardo Machado</a>. Contemporaries claimed he was drawn in mainly because he was the only sergeant with a car. He then became army chief of staff with the rank of colonel and controlled the armed forces behind a short-lived five-member presidency. In January 1934 he, conspiring with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> envoy Sumner Welles, forced President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramon-grau-san-martin">Ramón Grau San Martín</a> to resign, and he ruled through puppet presidents until he was elected president in 1940. He carried out social reforms and pro-union policies, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuba">Cuba</a> entered the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> on the side of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allies">Allies</a> on 9 December 1941.</p><p>After four years in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> he returned, and on 10 March 1952, three months before an election in which he stood a distant third, he seized power with army backing and cancelled the vote. He suspended the 1940 constitution and revoked most political liberties, including the right to strike. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/havana">Havana</a> became a centre of gambling and prostitution run for the American Mafia, and United States companies owned large shares of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuba">Cuba</a>'s sugar lands, mines and utilities. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fidel-castro">Fidel Castro</a>'s attack on the Moncada Barracks on 26 July 1953, Batista relied increasingly on police violence. Estimates of those killed range from hundreds to 20,000, a figure that is disputed.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>From December 1956 to December 1958 Castro's 26th of July Movement waged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrilla-warfare">guerrilla</a> war against the government. Rebels under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/che-guevara">Che Guevara</a> defeated Batista's forces at Santa Clara on New Year's Eve 1958, and on 1 January 1959 Batista fled to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dominican-republic">Dominican Republic</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rafael-trujillo">Rafael Trujillo</a>'s protection. He later found asylum in Portugal, living on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madeira">Madeira</a> and in Estoril, and died of a heart attack in Marbella, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, on 6 August 1973.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Fulgencio Batista” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In August 1933 an army revolt joined a general strike and sugar workers' uprisings to drive Machado out, and in September the Sergeants' Revolt, led by Sergeant <b>Fulgencio Batista</b>, overthrew Carlos Manuel de Céspedes y Quesada and the Pentarchy that followed.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-cuba-1902-1959">Republic of Cuba (1902-1959) · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Dictator <b>Fulgencio Batista</b> fled Havana in the early hours, handing victory to Fidel Castro's guerrillas.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/january-1">1 January in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Fidel Castro's 26th of July Movement overthrew <b>Fulgencio Batista</b>, who fled on 1 January 1959.</blockquote><a href="/state/republic-of-cuba">Republic of Cuba</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Banes, in what is now <a href="/countries/cuba">Cuba</a>. Died in Marbella, in what is now <a href="/countries/spain">Spain</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=20.96,-75.72&amp;year=1947" aria-label="Banes on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Fulgencio Batista"><image href="/maps/globes/g270n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6128 5699h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6128 5699h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=20.96,-75.72&amp;year=1947">What was here in 1947? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
